#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""share: put pictures and videos on the web and print a link to paste.

    share PATH [PATH ...]    upload, wait until the link works, print one link per file
    share --list             every file that is up, with its link
    share --rm TARGET ...    take files down (a link, a 10-character id or a file name)
    share --setup            store Cloudflare R2 settings; from then on files go there

Files go to one of two places. Both serve them at <base>/f/<id>/<name> with the right content
type, so a video plays in the browser and a picture opens in place, and your friends need no
account. The id is the start of the file's SHA-256, so sharing the same file again gives the same
link.

  * Cloudflare R2, once `share --setup` has stored its settings in ~/.config/share/r2.json. A
    bucket behind your own domain serves a file the moment its upload ends. Up to 5 TB a file; one
    over 100 MB goes up in parts, and if the connection drops, the next run goes on from the last part.
  * Otherwise a public GitHub repo of your own (default <you>/share, made on first use with `gh`),
    served by GitHub Pages at https://<you>.github.io/share/. A link works only once Pages has
    deployed it, usually 15 to 25 seconds after the push, and this waits for it. Up to 100 MB a
    file (git refuses more) and about 1 GB for the whole site. SHARE_BACKEND=github picks it even
    when R2 is set up, and SHARE_REPO=owner/name picks another repo.

Things to know:
  * Anyone with a link can open the file, and anyone can browse the GitHub repo. Never share what
    you would not post openly.
  * Browsers play mp4 (H.264) and webm inline; .mov, .mkv and .avi may download instead.
  * --rm on R2 deletes the file at once. With a Cache Purge token in the settings it also clears
    Cloudflare's cache, so the link stops working at once; without one the cache may serve the file
    for up to an hour. A browser that already opened it may keep showing it either way.
    On GitHub it rewrites the repo into one fresh commit and force-pushes, so the file leaves the
    history and stops counting toward the 1 GB, but GitHub may still serve the old commit by its
    SHA until it cleans up. Anything truly sensitive should not go up at all.

Links go to stdout, everything else to stderr, so `share clip.mp4 | wl-copy` works. On Wayland the
links are also copied to the clipboard.
"""

